{"skill":{"slug":"obsidian-ontology-sync","displayName":"Obsidian Ontology Sync"},"version":{"version":"1.0.1","createdAt":1772195130337,"changelog":"Security update: Removed all sensitive personal/company/project information from examples and replaced with generic placeholders","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":2608,"sha256":"4b0c1dba1f1ba2f0b6ca59bd295fa7adfade077724295667d4466b06407157ae","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":14594,"sha256":"851cc44393e9df232b1552e2b00d3c63d32566ea52736924f69f3d71718aff0c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/sync.py","size":13584,"sha256":"0516d6cf3630008b2db254e0ff9c9919201b4b9135f02e99fe69fbaef53c3966","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2383,"sha256":"47f061e9094bd7c8a31274d85ef0efd6441fdcff1042a7fbe01eecef7e0e6727","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789089249617,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"02272f73c39ca6fdd7b07bf04c2712a5f603fc676459d00f3befe0492756e67b","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/02272f73c39ca6fdd7b07bf04c2712a5f603fc676459d00f3befe0492756e67b","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: obsidian-ontology-sync\nVersion: 1.0.1\n\nThe skill's code (`scripts/sync.py`) and documentation (`SKILL.md`, `README.md`) are aligned with its stated purpose of synchronizing Obsidian notes with an ontology. The Python script performs local file system operations (reading markdown, writing JSONL files) and text processing using standard libraries. There is no evidence of data exfiltration, malicious execution (e.g., `os.system`, network calls), persistence mechanisms beyond its stated cron job setup for legitimate scheduling, or prompt injection attempts against the OpenClaw agent. All file access and operations are confined to the user's specified Obsidian vault and ontology storage paths.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779167659921},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":25,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":8,"checkedAt":1789089249617},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill does what it claims, but it automatically turns private Obsidian notes into persistent, searchable files containing contact and team-behavior data without enough privacy, retention, or access-control safeguards.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Reading configured private note directories and writing generated graph and feedback files is purpose-aligned, but duplicating emails, phone numbers, company links, source paths, response patterns, blockers, and reporting relationships into machine-queryable files is high-impact and under-contained.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No hidden installer or remote payload was found, but the documentation references helper scripts such as init.py and setup-cron.py that are not present in the artifact, and recommends cron-based automatic execution.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill names source directories and outputs, but it encourages broad recurring ingestion of PKM folders and team/daily-status notes without explicit least-privilege boundaries, exclude controls, consent guidance, or separate opt-in for personnel behavior analytics.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The script creates persistent output directories, appends extracted records to graph.jsonl, and writes feedback markdown. It does not set restrictive file permissions, define retention/deletion behavior, or provide confirmation before non-dry-run writes.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is coherent: scan Obsidian markdown, extract entities and relationships, write an ontology graph, analyze it, and generate feedback. The same capability intentionally processes sensitive contact, business, project, and team-status data.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable with private Obsidian content, including contacts and team-status information, being copied into persistent searchable files. Use a narrow config, run dry-run first, avoid syncing sensitive or employee-monitoring notes unless authorized, protect the output directory with restrictive permissions, and review or purge graph.jsonl and feedback reports regularly.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: The privacy findings are supported in substance: extraction of emails, phones, organizations, projects, response patterns, and behavioral/team-status data is disclosed as core functionality, but privacy, consent, retention, and generated-output handling are not adequately addressed.\n[LP3] unexpected: The least-privilege concern is supported: the skill describes broad note scanning, generated outputs, cron automation, and possible future bidirectional edits, but does not define strict tool or filesystem permission boundaries.\n[T09] expected: The static finding is supported in context: scripts/sync.py appends note-derived contact data to graph.jsonl in plaintext. This is aligned with the ontology purpose, but the lack of restrictive file mode and append-only retention is a real exposure risk rather than evidence of malware.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789089249617}}}}}